<h1 style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    Lesson Planner – Theme # My Personal Goals for the Future Include... – Lesson # 2
</h1>
<p style="text-align: center; margin-top: 10px; font-style: italic;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 8th Grade (CEFR: A2.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Future Plans and Goals</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> My Personal Goals for the Future Include...</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Analyze the structure of various texts. Reading: Interpret characters' motives in short stories. Phonemic Awareness: Demonstrate knowledge of phonemic awareness by practicing minimal pair sounds.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can analyze the structure of texts related to future education planning (e.g., "Find the introduction and conclusion in this study guide"). Can interpret characters' motives in stories about planning for the future (e.g., "Why does the student want to study science"). Can identify and produce the minimal pair sounds to enhance their pronunciation and sound discrimination skills (e.g., /θ/ in "math" and /s/ in "mass").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 10px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages
</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a collage of images depicting various career paths and educational opportunities (e.g., a university campus, a scientist in a lab, an artist painting, a person in a business meeting). Students are asked to briefly read the short labels or descriptions under each image (e.g., "Science degree," "Start a business," "Art career"). The teacher prompts students with "Look at these pictures. What kind of *future* do you see for these people? What are their *goals*?" using the target vocabulary *future* and *goals* to activate prior knowledge from Lesson 1.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher points to one image (e.g., an engineer) and models reading a short sentence about it, emphasizing pronunciation: "This person *plans to* be an engineer. They *have to* study a lot of *math*." The teacher highlights the pronunciation of "have to" and then explicitly points out the /θ/ sound in "math" and models the contrast with /s/ in "mass" (e.g., "It's not 'mass', it's 'math'").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say 'I *have to* study,' is it a choice or something necessary?" (Necessary)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Does 'future' mean now or later?" (Later)</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Future Plans and Goals".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a short narrative text titled "Sofia's Ambitious Vision for the Future" (approximately 150-200 words) about a teenager outlining her educational and career *goals*. The text incorporates grammar such as "have to," "must," "I want to," "I plan to," future continuous, and a rich selection of target vocabulary: *future, goals, plan, business, career, opportunity, vision, development, achieve, prepare, set, ambitious, important, future-oriented, if, when*. The text is structured to have a clear introduction, body paragraphs about specific plans, and a conclusion summarizing her *vision*.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reading: Analyze the structure of texts and interpret characters' motives in short stories.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read the text silently for the first time.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Reading:</b> The teacher asks: "What is Sofia's main *goal* for the *future*?" (Students read to identify the general purpose of the text).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False Statements:</b> Students read the text again to determine if a series of statements are true or false based on Sofia's *plans*. (e.g., "Sofia *plans to* start a *business* after high school." "She *has to* study science.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students match phrases describing Sofia's *motives* (e.g., "to help her family") with the *actions* she *plans to* take (e.g., "study management").</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading texts focused on modals of necessity ("have to," "must"), WH-questions for goals, future continuous. Vocabulary: *future, goals, plan, business, career, opportunity, vision, development, achieve, prepare, set, ambitious, important, future-oriented, if, when, for, in, ambitiously, carefully*. Phonemic awareness: minimal pairs /θ/ and /s/.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Reading Practice (Cloze Activity):</b> Students receive a modified version of "Sofia's Ambitious Vision for the Future" with blanks. They must choose from a word bank to fill in missing target vocabulary (e.g., *goals, plan, ambitious, opportunity*) and grammar structures (e.g., *have to, will be working*). Example: "Sofia’s main _________ is to open her own _________." (goals, business)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Text Structure Analysis (Reading for Structure):</b> In pairs, students re-read the text. They are given prompts to analyze its structure: "Read and highlight the sentences that introduce Sofia's overall *vision* (introduction)." "Underline sentences that describe specific *plans* and how she *has to* *prepare* for them (body)." "Circle the sentence that summarizes her long-term *development* and conclusion." This activity helps them analyze text structure.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Minimal Pair Sound Discrimination (Reading & Identifying):</b> The teacher displays sentences containing minimal pairs /θ/ and /s/. Students read these sentences aloud or silently. For instance: "I think *math* is an *important* subject on my *path* to *success*." or "The *mass* of students *must* *think* about their *future*." Students identify and underline the words containing /θ/ and /s/ as they read them, practicing sound discrimination in context.</li>
                </ol></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Future Plans and Goals".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Future Goals Analysis & Interpretation." Students work in small groups (3-4 students). Each group receives a different short, anonymous "Personal Goal Statement" (e.g., from an imagined student application or a survey response), each approximately 100-120 words. These statements describe different students' *ambitious* *future* *plans*, using target vocabulary and grammar (e.g., "I *plan to* study law," "I *will be working* in the community," "I *have to* overcome challenges," "My *goals* include...").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Group Reading:</b> Each group reads their assigned "Personal Goal Statement" *carefully*.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Analysis & Interpretation:</b> Groups collaborate to analyze their text, focusing on:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Identifying the main *goals* and *vision* of the student (analyzing text content).</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Interpreting the student's *motives* for these goals (e.g., "Why does he *want to* *achieve* this?").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Identifying sentences that show how the text is structured (e.g., "This sentence acts as an introduction to their *plan*.").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Highlighting specific phrases related to *future* time (e.g., "In five years, I *will be developing* new technology," "When I graduate, I *plan to*...").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Discussing what the student *has to* do to *achieve* their *goals*.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Group Presentation (Reading Aloud & Explaining):</b> Each group presents their analysis to the class. They read key sentences from their text to support their interpretations of the student's *motives* and *goals*. They discuss the structure of the text they read, e.g., "The introduction of this student's *plan* is..." and "The conclusion shows their long-term *vision* for *development*." This promotes sociolinguistic competence by engaging in collaborative goal-setting discussions (about the fictional student's goals).</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during group analysis and presentation, focusing on students' ability to read, comprehend, and interpret text. Additionally, a short written response based on the reading task.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Individually, students select ONE of the "Personal Goal Statements" from the Production stage (either their group's or another group's). They *read* it again and complete a graphic organizer or write a short paragraph (3-4 sentences) summarizing:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The student's main *future goal*.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">One specific action they *plan to* take or *have to* do.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">One reason or *motive* for their *ambitious* goal.</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accuracy in identifying main ideas and details from the chosen text.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of target vocabulary (e.g., *goals, plan, future, achieve*) and grammar (e.g., "I *want to*," "I *have to*").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity in interpreting the character's *motives* and analyzing basic text structure elements.</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer on a small slip of paper:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most *important* *goal* or *plan* you read about today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What strategy did you use to help you interpret the characters' *motives* in the texts?"</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new *vocabulary* did I learn or practice today about *future goals* and *plans*?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was challenging about *reading* and *analyzing* the different personal goal statements?"</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What aspects of the reading activities (text structure analysis, motive interpretation) worked well? Were students able to handle the A2.3 text complexity?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional *scaffolds* are needed for students who struggled with identifying main ideas or interpreting characters' *motives*?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Was the phonemic awareness practice (/θ/ vs /s/) sufficiently integrated?</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> "In our next lesson (Lesson 3), we *will be speaking* more actively about our *own future plans* and *goals*, using the vocabulary and grammar we practiced in our reading today. We *will have to* use these expressions to discuss our *ambitious* *visions* with our classmates."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>